WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
Part 3. You will hear part of a discussion between two textile students about their

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:17.840
research into sustainable fashion. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.160
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.759
Shall we go through our research for our sustainable fashion project?

00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:52.959
Good idea. I think I've done enough reading now.

00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:57.759
Me too. I've learnt such a lot about what sustainability actually means.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.360
Same for me with ethical fashion. I didn't really appreciate the difference between

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.959
that and sustainable fashion before doing this research.

00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:11.599
I know. Most people use these terms interchangeably, but in fact,

00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:17.279
the difference is quite distinct when you think about it. Sustainable relates to the environment

00:01:17.279 --> 00:01:21.839
and ethical relates more to the way workers or animals are treated.

00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:27.839
I totally understand why people get confused though. There are so many other terms used,

00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:32.000
like eco-friendly, which is actually quite meaningless.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.480
And the way companies use these terms when describing their products doesn't help.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.279
They're often deliberately vague, I think, and don't provide enough information about

00:01:41.279 --> 00:01:43.519
how their products are made. Yes.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.000
It was interesting to read about the debates surrounding wool production

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.199
and how ethical and sustainable that is. It's generally considered to be sustainable

00:01:53.199 --> 00:01:57.519
because it's a natural product. And it also lasts a long time

00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:03.120
and can be recycled. All very positive. But I wasn't convinced by the argument

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.599
that wool production is sustainable because it doesn't use many chemicals.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.800
What about all the fungicides and insecticides used in sheep farming?

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.880
Good point. And I couldn't find any evidence for the claim about sheep farming being better

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.199
for the environment than cattle farming. No, they're both really bad.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:31.440
I read different reports about how unethical it is to even shear sheep. Some people say it's cruel,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.320
but as long as the sheep are kept in good condition, I can't see anything wrong with it.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:36.960
Me neither.

00:02:37.279 --> 00:02:40.160
Before you hear the rest of the discussion,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.520
you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:23.039
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:30.479
Shall we talk about some of the semi-synthetic new fabrics now?

00:03:30.479 --> 00:03:31.839
Okay, let's do that.

00:03:32.479 --> 00:03:36.639
Let's start with lyocell. I've been reading about that.

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:41.199
Yeah, that's the one produced from the pulp of eucalyptus trees, isn't it?

00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:44.960
Yes. And what happens with that is really impressive.

00:03:45.679 --> 00:03:51.279
Over 99% of dissolving agents used in the manufacturing process are used again.

00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:59.360
Yeah. Now, there are a few semi-synthetic fabrics that I'd never heard of. Like cupro, for example.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.479
Made from byproducts of the cotton industry to create a kind of vegan silk.

00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:08.559
But I'm not sure how sustainable this really is,

00:04:08.559 --> 00:04:12.880
as there are so many reports of pollution caused by the manufacturing process.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.559
It doesn't compare favorably with all the other sustainable fabrics we've looked at, no.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:21.920
Bamboo is one fabric we're all familiar with,

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.200
but I didn't know that it was only organic bamboo that's truly sustainable.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.440
Me neither. Apparently, the manufacturing process for a significant proportion of bamboo

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.239
is chemically quite intensive, which obviously can be quite damaging.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.640
Ecoviral is an example of a semi-synthetic fabric which is becoming really popular.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.040
Probably because manufacturing causes 50% fewer emissions

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.559
and takes up half as much energy as conventional fabrics.

00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:57.279
That saves production costs as well as being better for the environment.

00:04:57.279 --> 00:05:01.359
That's true. I think demand for cork will continue to grow.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.880
It works really well in vegan shoes and bags.

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:09.440
And it's the only fabric that's fundamentally sustainable.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.320
The cork trees it comes from are renewable,

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.160
and the product itself is both recyclable and biodegradable, which is unique.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.000
And the harvesting process is actually good for the trees.

00:05:22.559 --> 00:05:25.279
There are no downsides to using this source at all.

00:05:26.399 --> 00:05:30.959
Hemp is another really good sustainable fabric from a natural source.

00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:35.600
Yes. Did you know that cloves made from hemp protect the wearer from the sun?

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.640
And it's also antibacterial.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.440
No, I didn't. But I did read that it's quite hard to grow,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.559
so perhaps that's why it's not as common as you'd think.

00:05:46.559 --> 00:05:49.760
I'm sure that'll change. Then, there are...

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.359
That is the end of Part 3.

00:05:55.359 --> 00:06:00.480
You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

